    "narrative": "The offensive ceiling for Ghost Ride the WHIP today is anchored by **James Wood** (76.28), who benefits from a prime matchup against Giolito (5.37 xERA). His position at the top of the order combined with strong peripheral metrics (0.384 xwOBA) makes him our clear standout play. **Josh Naylor** (65.16) and **Austin Riley** (64.98) provide the necessary stability in the heart of the order; while both face respectable arms, their situational scores are bolstered by favorable park factors and the ability to capitalize on middle-of-the-road SP skill sets. **Corbin Carroll** (62.47) remains a mandatory start; despite a tough assignment against Kirby, his 87% stolen base success projection against a slow delivery makes him our primary engine for the scarcest category in this league.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was **Vinnie Pasquantino** (43.95). While he hovered near the 40.0 efficiency threshold, the heavy penalty for his platoon disadvantage against the lefty Gore effectively neutralized his base projection, pushing him behind the higher-upside configurations of **Tyler Soderstrom** and **Brice Turang**. We are also keeping a close eye on **Jose Caballero** (57.17); while he offers elite speed potential, his score is suppressed by a brutal platoon split and a low-order slot, necessitating caution despite the favorable matchup against Severino.\n\nInvestors should note that we are navigating significant risk against top-tier arms today. Both **Gabriel Moreno** and **Corbin Carroll** are locked into a difficult encounter with Kirby (3.46 xERA), which creates a potential drag on our AVG and R categories. Furthermore, **Cole Young** (44.12) is skating near the bottom of our playable floor against Gallen; his inclusion is strictly a volume play to protect our game caps, as his efficiency is heavily mitigated by the opposing pitcher's high skill level. We remain positioned to pivot if late-breaking weather reports or lineup shuffles impact **Travis Bazzana** or **Zach McKinstry**, both of whom rely heavily on their lead-off/late-order placement to justify their starts.",
